 "caption": "Where do you think Stonehenge's smaller stones came from? 🪨\n\nThe giant sarsens came from about 25 km away, but the smaller bluestones match no local rock. Geologists traced them to the Preseli Hills in west Wales, over 200 km (125 miles) from Stonehenge, and digs there found the outcrops where Stone Age people prised pillars loose more than 5,000 years ago. Each bluestone weighs several tonnes. Then in 2024 a study in Nature traced the Altar Stone to northeast Scotland, about 750 km (460 miles) away. How they were moved is still debated.\n\nSend this to the friend who thinks they know Stonehenge.\n\nImages: Wikimedia Commons and the Library of Congress (public domain / CC BY, credits in the comments). Sources: English Heritage, UCL, Nature (2024).",
